# What the worker image build may see.
#
# `deploy-brain.sh build` and `infra/docker-compose.dev.yaml` both pass the
# **repository root** as the build context, because `worker/Dockerfile`
# installs `docaget/` as a path dependency and needs both trees. Without this
# file that context is the whole checkout: measured 2026-09-10, **12 GB**, of
# which 9.8 GB is `app/src-tauri/target` — Rust build artifacts that no line of
# the Dockerfile can reach. On a disk with 11 GB free that is not waste, it is
# a deploy that can fill the volume, which this machine has already been
# bitten by once (`doc/COMPANY_BRAIN.md`, and the reason `--release` used to be
# mandatory here).
#
# **An allowlist, not a denylist**, and that is the decision worth keeping: the
# Dockerfile copies five explicit paths and nothing else, so anything that
# grows anywhere else in this repository — a new corpus, a second target
# directory, a bundled binary — cannot silently re-enter the context. A
# denylist would need editing every time, and the symptom of forgetting is a
# slow build rather than a broken one, which is the kind of thing nobody
# chases.
#
# Keep this in step with the `COPY` lines in `worker/Dockerfile` — five of them
# now that the read-only scripts ship too. There is no
# test that can: a build with a missing path fails loudly at `uv sync`, which
# is the guard, and it is a good one.
*

!docaget/pyproject.toml
!docaget/README.md
!docaget/docagent
!worker/pyproject.toml
!worker/brainworker
!worker/scripts

# Re-excluded inside what was let back in. `__pycache__` is the one that
# matters: it is regenerated in the image anyway, and a stale `.pyc` beside a
# source file is exactly the trap `doc/CLAUDE.md` records — CPython validates
# a cached bytecode on the source's integer-second mtime and its size, so a
# same-length edit can serve the code you thought you replaced.
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.venv
